¶
'''biglot''' (bī′glot), ''a.'' [< L. ''bi-'', two-, + Gr. γλῶττα, tongue.] In two languages; bilingual. ''N. E. D.'' [Rare.]

'''bigly†''' (big′li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''bigly'', powerfully, bravely; < ''big''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a tumid, swelling, blustering manner; haughtily; arrogantly.
{{smaller block|He brawleth ''bigly''. {{right|''Sir T. More'', Works, p. 701.}}}}

'''bigmouth''' (big′mouth), ''n.'' A fish of the family ''Centrarchidæ'', ''Chænobryttus gulosus''. Also called ''warmouth''. See cut under ''Centrarchidæ''.

'''bigness''' (big′nes), ''n.'' [< ''big''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ness''.] The state or quality of being big; largeness of proportions; size, whether large or small; bulk, absolute or relative.
{{smaller block|Hayle of suche ''bygnesse'' that it slewe both men and beestys. {{right|''Fabyan'', I. 238.}}
Their legs are both of a ''bigness''. ''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., ii. 4.
The ''bigness'' and uncouth deformity of the camel. {{right|''Sir R. L’Estrange.''}}
Large oak, walnut, hickory, ash, beech, poplar, and many other sorts of timber, of surprising ''bigness''. {{right|''Beverley'', Virginia, ii. ¶ 2.}}}}

'''Bignonia''' (big-nō′ni-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., named after ''Bignon'', librarian to Louis XV.] A genus of plants of many species, natural order ''Bignoniaceæ'', natives of the warmer portions of the new world. {{smaller|The species are characterized by a twining or climbing stem, frequently in the tropics reaching the tops of the highest trees, with divided leaves and often magnificent trumpet-shaped flowers. In the stems of some species the wood is so arranged as to have a cross-like appearance in section. The most northern species, ''B. capreolata'' of the southern United States, is frequently cultivated in gardens, and others are ornaments of greenhouses. ''B. Chica'' of South America yields an orange-red coloring matter called ''chico'' (which see).}}

